{
  "statement": "The biggest risk to this MVP isn't bad code. It's not shipping. An established consumer software team with 90 days to validate a product hypothesis faces an asymmetric bet: ship AI-generated prototype code and learn from real users, or hand-craft every line and learn nothing until day 89. Facebook launched on PHP so ugly it became an industry punchline — then used the revenue from 6 million early users to build HipHop and eventually Hack. The prototype wasn't the product. The prototype was the instrument that told them what the product should be. Every day your MVP sits in a staging environment is a day your competitor is collecting user behavior data you'll never recover. For a low-risk consumer product with no safety-critical features, the downside of imperfect code is bounded — some user churn, a moderate refactor bill. The downside of silence from the market is unbounded — you never learn whether your hypothesis was right.\n\nHere's the part nobody wants to say out loud: human developers are already the dominant source of technical debt in every established codebase. Stripe's internal analysis found that developers spend roughly 33% of their time dealing with technical debt — debt authored entirely by humans. Every company with more than five years of code history has modules nobody understands because the original author left in 2019. The \"knowledge void\" that critics pin on AI-generated code? It already exists. It's called employee turnover. The difference is that AI-generated code can be run through static analysis, automated test generation, and documentation tooling at the moment of creation — not six months later when someone finally gets around to the backlog. Blaming AI for a maintenance problem that predates AI by decades is like blaming email for miscommunication.\n\nThe smart move is proportional adaptive safety — not a front-loaded audit gate that eats your timeline. Allocate 5–7% of your 90-day sprint capacity to automated test generation for AI-produced modules, real-time error telemetry, and rollback triggers tied to feature flags. An established company already owns this infrastructure: CI/CD pipelines, A/B testing frameworks, error monitoring dashboards. You're not building new safety apparatus — you're pointing existing apparatus at new code. That 5–7% costs you roughly 4–6 engineering days across the sprint. The alternative — a two-week upfront code audit — costs you 15% of your timeline and generates zero market data. Finance should love this math. Engineering should love the coverage. Product should love the velocity. The three incentives align when you measure outcomes instead of gatekeeping inputs.\n\nThe \"terminal maintenance burden\" scenario requires a very specific organizational failure: you ship the prototype, you collect no data, you learn nothing, and you maintain dead code forever. That failure mode is identical whether a human or an AI wrote the code. The fix is iteration discipline, not authorship policing. Plan the refactor sprint before you ship the prototype sprint. Budget 20–30% of post-MVP engineering capacity for targeted rewrites informed by actual usage patterns. Instagram's initial MVP was a Python/Django monolith that couldn't scale past a few million users — they refactored after acquisition with the resources that early traction unlocked. The code didn't trap them. Stagnation would have. AI-generated prototype code converts to validated learning the moment real users touch it. The only thing that converts it to terminal burden is a team that stops iterating. So the question isn't whether to use AI code in your 90-day sprint — it's whether you have the discipline to keep learning after you ship. And if you don't have that discipline, hand-crafted code won't save you either.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🏎️", "tooltip": "A race car doesn't win by idling in the pit — the MVP's value comes from laps on the track, not perfection in the garage."},
