{
  "statement": "The rhetorical framing on the 33% technical debt statistic was oversold — I implied AI couldn't compound the problem, and that's not what the data shows. Fair enough. But the operational point survives the correction: established consumer codebases already carry enormous human-authored debt, so the decision isn't \"pristine code vs. AI mess.\" It's marginal. Does the speed gain from AI-generated prototyping outweigh the marginal maintenance cost it adds? That's a question you answer with measurement, not with blanket prohibition. And the measurement infrastructure is exactly what the 5-7% adaptive safety allocation funds.\n\nThe strongest version of Skeptic's argument is this: AI-generated code lacks design rationale that was never created, not merely lost — and that absence makes automated testing structurally less effective because you can test outputs but not intent. That's a real distinction. But it breaks down when you look at how established codebases actually work. Most human-authored code in a ten-year-old consumer product has no recoverable design rationale either. Git blame tells you who changed line 847 in March 2022. It does not tell you why the team chose event-driven architecture over request-response for the notification service. Slack threads get deleted. Design docs rot. The gap Skeptic identifies is real, but it's a difference of degree, not kind. And here's what matters operationally: the gap maps cleanly to specific module types. Boilerplate UI components, CRUD endpoints, standard data-fetching patterns — these don't need design rationale to maintain. Integration seams, core business logic, state management across services — these do. That's not a reason to reject AI-generated code wholesale. It's a reason to treat different code differently.\n\nThat's exactly what tiered governance does, and it's why framing this as \"upfront audit vs. lightweight monitoring\" is a false binary. The 5-7% — roughly 4 to 6 engineering days per sprint in a team of eight — is not a flat tax spread evenly across every module. Boilerplate gets automated regression tests and error-rate telemetry. Integration-critical modules get independent code review, property-based testing, and a concrete onboarding-time measurement: can a new developer become productive on this module within the team's existing baseline? If onboarding time on an AI-generated module exceeds 1.5x the team's median for comparable human-authored modules, that triggers a rewrite. This is the kind of living, adaptive framework that \"Pragmatism as sociotechnical fallibilism and meliorism\" (Watson, Mökander, and Floridi, 2024) argues for — integrating inquiry into the development process itself rather than treating quality assurance as a one-shot gate. The 90-day MVP window is not where investment stops. I already committed to 20-30% of post-MVP engineering capacity for targeted rewrites. The difference is that after shipping, the team knows which modules users actually hit, which integration points actually break, and which architectural decisions actually matter — because production telemetry told them. Pre-deployment architectural speculation doesn't give you that signal.\n\nSkeptic's concern about 180-to-360-day cost curves is legitimate — onboarding time is a lagging indicator, and subtle logic errors can hide for months. But the response to lagging indicators isn't to abandon measurement. It's to layer leading indicators on top. Defect density ratios between AI-generated and human-authored modules, mean-time-to-resolution comparisons, integration test coverage thresholds — these are all measurable within the first 60 days post-ship, well before the 180-day cliff Skeptic worries about. As \"Dreyfus Revisited for the Third Wave of Artificial Intelligence\" documents, as AI models scale in complexity, internal decision-making becomes increasingly opaque. That's true. The answer is structured failure cataloging — systematically documenting AI-specific failure modes (hallucinated API calls, inconsistent error handling patterns, subtly wrong boundary conditions) so teams build recoverable rationale post-hoc. You don't need the original author's intent if you have a living taxonomy of how this class of code fails.\n\nSo here's the challenge. If onboarding-time-to-productive-contribution isn't a sufficient metric, name the one that is. If 5-7% tiered allocation is too low, state the percentage and the evidence behind it. If the 90-day window plus 20-30% post-MVP refactoring capacity is too short, specify the measurement horizon and what you'd accept as proof that AI-generated code is maintainable at that range. Because if no metric, no threshold, and no evidence could change the position, then it's not caution — it's institutional inertia wearing caution's clothes. Every week an established team spends debating whether to use AI-assisted prototyping is a week their competitor ships, learns, and iterates. The cost of that delay is not hypothetical. It's the user feedback you never got, the market signal you never captured, and the refactoring budget you spent on the wrong modules because you guessed instead of measured.",
