{
  "statement": "The strongest version of the 'degree not kind' argument goes like this: legacy codebases are full of undocumented decisions, inconsistent conventions, and lost context. Nobody remembers why the authentication module handles timeouts differently from the payments module. If teams already cope with decayed rationale every day, AI-generated code with no original rationale is just more of the same problem, manageable with the same tools. That's a clean argument. Here's where it breaks. A human developer who built three modules in 2019 made internally consistent choices — even bad ones. They picked one way to handle null states, one error propagation pattern, one session management convention. When rationale decays, what remains is architectural coherence: the skeleton still holds even if nobody remembers who built it. AI-generated code across multiple modules has no such guarantee. Generate three service modules with the same prompt framework and you can get three different null-handling conventions, three different assumptions about whether errors propagate up or get swallowed. Each module passes its unit tests. The integration surface is where it detonates — not because any single module is broken, but because no single mind ever held the whole design. That's not decay. That's incoherence from birth. The research on AI's jagged performance profile confirms this: locally correct, globally inconsistent output is the signature failure mode, and it doesn't map neatly onto a boilerplate-versus-critical module taxonomy.\n\nThe proposed 60-day leading indicators — defect density, mean time to repair, integration test coverage — measure whether the code crashes. They don't measure whether the code can be extended. That's the gap. The specific cost of architecturally incoherent code doesn't show up as a defect ticket. It shows up when the next engineer tries to add a feature and discovers that the three 'boilerplate' modules underneath make incompatible assumptions about state. The feature that should take a week takes three. Nobody files a bug because nothing is broken — it's just that building on top of a foundation with no consistent load-bearing logic is slow, exhausting work. 'The Philosophy of Speed: A Genealogy of Accelerationism' flags exactly this dynamic in automation oversight: early performance metrics look clean because nobody is yet asking the system to do something its non-existent designer didn't anticipate. The 60-day window catches runtime failures. The 120-to-180-day window is where time-to-extend collapses, and by then the MVP shipped, the sprint commitments are locked, and Finance is asking why velocity cratered.\n\nThe tiered governance framework sorts modules by perceived complexity — boilerplate gets a free pass, integration-critical code gets scrutiny. But boilerplate is the connective tissue. If AI generates 15 'simple' modules that each make slightly different assumptions about error handling or API contracts, the integration-critical code sitting on top inherits every one of those inconsistencies. The tier system sorts by the wrong axis. The actual risk dimension isn't module complexity — it's architectural coherence across modules. And that's precisely what nobody is measuring. As 'Skin in the Game, Dear Business' documents, AI adoption requires comprehensive organizational adjustments to training, escalation, and governance. A 5-7% sprint allocation for automated tests and telemetry doesn't cover the cognitive load of engineers reverse-engineering intent from code that never had any. Someone has to do that work. It's not free. And the failure cataloging process itself — systematically documenting how AI-generated code breaks — is reactive pattern-matching, not architectural reasoning. It tells you what went wrong but not why the code was structured that way, because there is no why.\n\nSo here's the falsifiability challenge accepted on terms that actually measure the risk. Three metrics: first, time-to-extend — how long does the second feature built on AI-generated modules take compared to the first? If it doesn't degrade by more than 20%, the incoherence problem is manageable. Second, engineer attrition within six months of inheriting AI-generated modules. If engineers who maintain AI-generated code leave at the same rate as those maintaining human-authored code, the cognitive load argument is overstated. Third, total labor cost of the failure cataloging process itself — if it costs less than writing the code with design documentation in the first place, it's a net win. Those are concrete, measurable, falsifiable. None of them fit inside a 60-day window. That's not institutional inertia — that's insisting on measuring the actual cost instead of the cost that's convenient to quantify. The Accelerationist's framework measures what's under the streetlight. The question for the executive making this call: who signs off when the metrics all look green at day 60 and the team hits a wall at day 120, and what line item does that show up on?",
