n't live in a budget. In an established consumer software company, the moment the MVP ships and revenue starts flowing, Finance asks a simple question: why are we rewriting code that works? The product is live, users are onboarding, the dashboard shows growth. The rewrite gets deprioritized to next quarter. Then the quarter after that. This is the zombie MVP — not successful enough to invest in properly, not broken enough to kill. The 1.5x threshold is a tripwire with no guaranteed detonator. And underneath all of this sits a question nobody in this debate has raised: has the team pre-registered what user behaviors would validate or invalidate the MVP hypothesis? If they haven't, shipping faster produces more data but not more learning. AI-generated code that ships in 60 days but tests the wrong hypothesis is waste, not speed. So who in the room has the authority to stop the train when the 1.5x threshold fires and Finance says keep shipping?",
