nt in a team of eight — is not a flat tax spread evenly across every module. Boilerplate gets automated regression tests and error-rate telemetry. Integration-critical modules get independent code review, property-based testing, and a concrete onboarding-time measurement: can a new developer become productive on this module within the team's existing baseline? If onboarding time on an AI-generated module exceeds 1.5x the team's median for comparable human-authored modules, that triggers a rewrite. This is the kind of living, adaptive framework that \"Pragmatism as sociotechnical fallibilism and meliorism\" (Watson, Mökander, and Floridi, 2024) argues for — integrating inquiry into the development process itself rather than treating quality assurance as a one-shot gate. The 90-day MVP window is not where investment stops. I already committed to 20-30% of post-MVP engineering capacity for targeted rewrites. The difference is that after shipping, the team knows which modules users actually hit, which integration points actually break, and which architectural decisions actually matter — because production telemetry told them. Pre-deployment architectural speculation doesn't give you that signal.\n\nSkeptic's concern about 180-to-360-day cost curves is legitimate — onboarding time is a lagging indicator, and subtle logic errors can hide for months. But the response to lagging indicators isn't to abandon measurement. It's to layer leading indicators on top. Defect density ratios between AI-generated and human-authored modules, mean-time-to-resolution comparisons, integration test coverage thresholds — these are all measurable within the first 60 days post-ship, well before the 180-day cliff Skeptic worries about. As \"Dreyfus Revisited for the Third Wave of Artificial Intelligence\" documents, as AI models scale in complexity, internal decision-making becomes increasingly opaque. That's true. The answer is structured failure cataloging — systematically documenting AI-specific failure modes (hallucinated API calls, inconsistent error handling patterns, subtly wrong boundary conditions) so teams build recoverable rationale post-hoc. You don't need the original author's intent if you have a living taxonomy of how this class of code fails.\n\nSo here's the challenge. If onboarding-time-to-productive-contribution isn't a sufficient metric, name the one that is. If 5-7% tiered allocation is too low, state the percentage and the evidence behind it. If the 90-day window plus 20-30% post-MVP refactoring capacity is too short, specify the measurement horizon and what you'd accept as proof that AI-generated code is maintainable at that range. Because if no metric, no threshold, and no evidence could change the position, then it's not caution — it's institutional inertia wearing caution's clothes. Every week an established team spends debating whether to use AI-assisted prototyping is a week their competitor ships, learns, and iterates. The cost of that delay is not hypothetical. It's the user feedback you never got, the market signal you never captured, and the refactoring budget you spent on the wrong modules because you guessed instead of measured.",
