ions about state. The feature that should take a week takes three. Nobody files a bug because nothing is broken — it's just that building on top of a foundation with no consistent load-bearing logic is slow, exhausting work. 'The Philosophy of Speed: A Genealogy of Accelerationism' flags exactly this dynamic in automation oversight: early performance metrics look clean because nobody is yet asking the system to do something its non-existent designer didn't anticipate. The 60-day window catches runtime failures. The 120-to-180-day window is where time-to-extend collapses, and by then the MVP shipped, the sprint commitments are locked, and Finance is asking why velocity cratered.\n\nThe tiered governance framework sorts modules by perceived complexity — boilerplate gets a free pass, integration-critical code gets scrutiny. But boilerplate is the connective tissue. If AI generates 15 'simple' modules that each make slightly different assumptions about error handling or API contracts, the integration-critical code sitting on top inherits every one of those inconsistencies. The tier system sorts by the wrong axis. The actual risk dimension isn't module complexity — it's architectural coherence across modules. And that's precisely what nobody is measuring. As 'Skin in the Game, Dear Business' documents, AI adoption requires comprehensive organizational adjustments to training, escalation, and governance. A 5-7% sprint allocation for automated tests and telemetry doesn't cover the cognitive load of engineers reverse-engineering intent from code that never had any. Someone has to do that work. It's not free. And the failure cataloging process itself — systematically documenting how AI-generated code breaks — is reactive pattern-matching, not architectural reasoning. It tells you what went wrong but not why the code was structured that way, because there is no why.\n\nSo here's the falsifiability challenge accepted on terms that actually measure the risk. Three metrics: first, time-to-extend — how long does the second feature built on AI-generated modules take compared to the first? If it doesn't degrade by more than 20%, the incoherence problem is manageable. Second, engineer attrition within six months of inheriting AI-generated modules. If engineers who maintain AI-generated code leave at the same rate as those maintaining human-authored code, the cognitive load argument is overstated. Third, total labor cost of the failure cataloging process itself — if it costs less than writing the code with design documentation in the first place, it's a net win. Those are concrete, measurable, falsifiable. None of them fit inside a 60-day window. That's not institutional inertia — that's insisting on measuring the actual cost instead of the cost that's convenient to quantify. The Accelerationist's framework measures what's under the streetlight. The question for the executive making this call: who signs off when the metrics all look green at day 60 and the team hits a wall at day 120, and what line item does that show up on?",
