. This composite approach absorbs the legitimate concern about early metrics creating false confidence — because it doesn't rely on any single metric. If defect density looks clean at day 60 but cross-module extension time is 2x baseline, that's a red flag the pipeline catches. The question isn't whether to measure — it's whether to measure early or measure late. And measuring late means shipping an MVP on code you've never stress-tested for extensibility, then discovering the problem when your post-launch feature velocity craters.\n\nOne gap remains conspicuous: the 1.5x onboarding-time threshold has been on the table since my earlier turn, and the counter-metrics proposed in response — time-to-extend, attrition, failure-cataloging cost — are all 120-plus-day measurements. None of them explain why a deliberate cross-module feature assignment at day 60-75 would fail to surface the incoherence being described. If an engineer hits inconsistent null-handling across three modules, that shows up in completion time whether the assignment is organic or deliberate. The mechanism of discovery doesn't change the signal. So the direct challenge stands: does the cross-module extension metric at day 60-75, paired with the 1.5x threshold, catch the problem — or does it not? If it does, the temporal disagreement collapses and we're arguing about calibration, not architecture. If it doesn't, I need to hear the specific failure mode that a deliberate cross-module probe at day 60 would miss but organic discovery at day 150 would catch.",
