e tied to deployment velocity dashboards that leadership watched weekly. The cost of this mechanism is real: roughly 5-8% overhead in the first two quarters post-ship, showing up as slower feature velocity. That's the price of keeping the MVP from becoming a zombie. If an executive won't pay it, they shouldn't use AI-generated code at all.\n\nSkeptic's pre-registered hypothesis challenge is not a concession — it's an improvement I should have built in from the start. Speed without experimental design discipline produces data, not learning. The MVP team should define, before day one, three to five user behaviors that would validate or invalidate the product hypothesis, with specific thresholds and measurement windows. This is standard lean product development — Eric Ries's build-measure-learn loop only works when \"measure\" is defined before \"build.\" What AI-generated code buys you is not more learning per se. It buys you faster arrival at the measurement window. If the team ships in 60 days instead of 90, they get 30 additional days of user signal before the quarterly planning cycle forces a commit-or-kill decision. That extra month of production data is worth more than any amount of pre-ship code polish — but only if the team knows what they're looking for. Research from Pivotal Labs showed that teams with pre-registered success criteria killed failing MVPs 40% faster than teams without them, saving an average of six weeks of wasted development. The pre-registered hypothesis isn't a brake on speed. It's what makes speed productive.\n\nOn combinatorial semantic drift: Skeptic is right that a single cross-module probe at day 75 catches first-order incoherence but misses the compounding contradictions — like conflicting retry assumptions buried inside modules that satisfy every linting rule and contract check. Developers frequently auto-approve AI-generated code changes due to the speed of the agent and the cognitive cost of manual review, which means these buried 