tion begins within one sprint, the organizational mechanism failed regardless of the pipeline's technical merit. That is a falsifiable commitment, not a hope.\n\nSkeptic's pre-registered hypothesis challenge is not a concession — it is an upgrade. Speed without experimental design discipline produces data, not learning. Before sprint one, the product team pre-registers three to five user behaviors that would validate or invalidate the MVP hypothesis, with quantitative thresholds — for example, ≥20% day-7 retention or ≥15% activation of the core feature loop. AI-generated code gets the experiment into users' hands faster, but the experiment has to be designed to teach something. Developers frequently auto-approve AI-generated code changes due to the speed of output and the cognitive cost of manual review, as documented in research on agentic system interactions. That auto-approval tendency means the pre-registered criteria do double duty: they discipline both the product hypothesis and the code review process, because code that doesn't serve a registered learning goal gets flagged, not rubber-stamped.\n\nThe combinatorial drift problem — contradictory retry assumptions buried inside modules that pass every linting rule and contract check — is real and my original single-probe design was insufficient. The fix is iteration, not delay. Run new cross-module comprehensibility assignments monthly after day 75, rotating the engineer-module pairing each time. If the composite comprehensibility score degrades across two consecutive monthly probes, that triggers the same rewrite escalation as the 1.5x threshold. The total overhead of doing this responsibly — 5–7% sprint allocation for automated governance, 20–30% post-ship rewrite capacity, monthly rotating probes — is 28–41% of post-ship engineering capacity. That is the honest price of speed, and any executive who thinks they can get AI-assisted velocity for free is buying a zombie MVP.\n\nThe single sharpest remaining disag